# f1 = byName, f2 = byValue, f3 = attempt, f4 = Knob, f6 = spin, f7 = Latch
# `this.turn = this.turn.bind(this)` in the constructor of a class: a name that the prototype answers by a method and that every object holds itself.
# No name that something above answers could be a field: the bound function lay beside the fields, every look at the object failed (HasNoExtraProperties), the read and the call went by stubs.
# Where the store stands on every way through the constructor of the class itself, the name is an appended field. A store under a condition (Latch) appends nothing: a slot left empty would send every call of the method to the closed arm.
# Who finds a method by the class alone asks the appended fields as it asks the listed ones: the method table has no row for such a layout, the search for a member above gives up.
# What is held in the field, or in a variable that was read out of it, is a bound function of the method: the call asks the cell and enters the method.
# RUN(FIELDS): link fields mechanisms=all
# FIELDS: field L0.1 "turn" appended
# FIELDS-NOT: field L1.1 "turn"
# The program itself stores another function over the name and deletes it; the shell does what it likes to the object and to the prototype between the runs.
